---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import LearningDiagram from '@/components/modelling/LearningDiagram.astro';
import LearningIllustration from '@/components/modelling/LearningIllustration.astro';
import { contexts, contextRoute, contextMap, candidateManifest } from '@/lib/property-pack-model.mjs';
const propertyContexts = contexts.filter((context) => context.kind === 'bounded-context');
---

<ModellingLayout title="Connect meanings across contexts" description="An exchange succeeds when the recipient understands the relevant meaning. Give definitions an accountable home and inspect exactly what a proposed correspondence preserves, loses or invents." proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document">
  <h2 id="meaning"><span id="same-label" aria-hidden="true"></span>The same word can be doing different work</h2>
  <p>“Completion” could label a transaction event or the finishing of a document-preparation task. “Property” could point to a dwelling, a building or a record of rights. A shared label is not enough to make those meanings interchangeable. The practical question is what a recipient will conclude after information crosses the boundary.</p>
  <p>A <a href="/glossary#bounded-context">bounded context</a> is an explicit boundary within which meanings and their ownership are maintained consistently. Its <a href="/glossary#semantic-home">semantic home</a> identifies responsibility for a definition. This is not permission to invent conflicting facts: it is a way to preserve legitimate differences in the questions a model answers.</p>

  <h2 id="boundary"><span id="discover-boundaries" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="contexts" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="homes" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="semantic-home" aria-hidden="true"></span>Work within the documented boundaries</h2>
  <p>The boundaries for this development phase are established inputs, not something each reader or workshop must rediscover. OPDA's maintained <a href="/development/property-pack/contexts#homes">contextual-boundary register</a> names these {propertyContexts.length} property semantic homes:</p>
  <ul>{propertyContexts.map((context) => <li><a href={contextRoute(context)}>{context.label}</a> — {context.definition}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>The <a href="/development/property-pack/contexts/common">Common boundary</a> has a different job: stable exchange meaning genuinely shared by several contexts. It stays deliberately small and is governed through the Interoperability Working Group. A term does not belong there merely because several applications use it, or because nobody has yet resolved its ownership.</p>
  <p>The DBT Smart Data scheme working group addresses scheme-level questions. Separately, the candidate register includes a <a href="/development/property-pack/contexts/dbt-smart-data">machine-proposed DBT Smart Data semantic context</a> for authorisation and controlled sharing. That candidate context is not the external government programme, not an OPDA working-group decision and not a seventh property-domain bounded context.</p>

  <LearningDiagram kind="boundaries" caption="Profession, working group, system and semantic home are related but distinct. None supplies a one-to-one rule for assigning all of a participant's knowledge to one context." />
  <p>A surveyor is a person with professional knowledge. A working group brings people together to review meanings. A software system may display survey reports, transaction records and listing information. A semantic home identifies responsibility for a particular meaning. The same professional can contribute evidence to several questions, and the same system can use meanings from several homes.</p>
  <p>For example, someone working on a property-data platform may identify an ambiguity in a lending participation record. Their employer's product does not automatically move that meaning into Property Technology. Follow the documented ownership and ask the responsible people to review the distinction. Within a context, resolve contradictory definitions; do not use a mapping to disguise a disagreement in its own language.</p>

  <h2 id="mapping-example">Compare full definitions before drawing a bridge</h2>
  <p>The following is a fictional vocabulary comparison, not a mapping in the candidate. Both lists concern physical access during one identified visit to the same nonempty set of identified agreed areas, and use the same meaning of “accessed”. Their scopes differ in one important respect.</p>
  <div class="learning-spread">
    <section class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="source-choice">
      <h3 id="source-choice">Source vocabulary · teaching version 1</h3>
      <p><strong>Some access:</strong> at least one, but not all, of the identified agreed areas was accessed.</p>
      <p>Includes access to two of three areas. Excludes access to none and access to all.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="recipient-choice">
      <h3 id="recipient-choice">Recipient vocabulary · teaching version 1</h3>
      <p><strong>Restricted access:</strong> not all of the identified agreed areas were accessed.</p>
      <p>Includes access to two of three areas and access to none. Excludes access to all.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <LearningDiagram kind="mapping" caption="A deliberately limited fictional correspondence: ‘some access’ has narrower meaning than ‘restricted access’, which also includes none. The connection is not an accepted candidate mapping." />
  <p>Every “some access” case meets the recipient's “restricted access” definition under these premises. The reverse does not hold: a “restricted” record could mean zero areas were accessed. Treating the concepts as exact substitutes would lose that distinction in one direction and invent some access in the other.</p>
  <p>An exact correspondence requires the relevant meanings to support that strong substitution. A close correspondence says the concepts are related enough for a stated use while remaining different; it is not simply an exact match with a lower confidence score. Broader and narrower correspondence express direction of scope. No correspondence may be justified when subjects differ or the evidence is insufficient.</p>
  <p>Record the definitions and versions compared, the intended use, counterexamples and the reason for the chosen relationship. An agreement about version 1 does not automatically cover a later definition that adds or removes cases. A useful mapping is a reviewable claim with consequences, not a line drawn because two labels look friendly.</p>

  <h2 id="harbour-court"><span id="property-pack" aria-hidden="true"></span>Keep the date the recipient actually needs</h2>
  <p>Harbour Court's source account identifies an inspection on 12 August 2026, report version 1 issued on 14 August and version 2 on 20 August. Suppose a fictional recipient's definition of “inspection occurrence date” is “the calendar date on which the identified inspection took place”. A proposal fills it with the latest report issue date.</p>
  <p>The proposal supplies 20 August where the source and recipient meanings require 12 August. It does not preserve the requested meaning, even if both values are dates and the recipient successfully reads the record. The agreed exchange must retain the inspection reference, the occurrence date and the report version supporting the account. It must not redefine a report correction as another visit.</p>

  <section class="learning-practice" aria-labelledby="transfer">
    <h2 id="transfer">Practice: when “completion” does not correspond</h2>
    <LearningIllustration scene="conveyancing" alt="Two distinct document bundles with separate event markers and a title-boundary drawing, keeping their dated subjects apart." />
    <p>In fictional glossary A version 1, “completion” means the transaction-transfer event identified as C in transaction T. In fictional task list B version 1, “completion” means the event of finishing preparation of document bundle D. The teaching record explicitly states that preparation finished on 7 September and C occurred on 10 September. These definitions do not claim to set professional or legal terminology.</p>
    <h3>Worked response</h3>
    <p>The supplied definitions identify different events, so an exact correspondence is not supported. Retain the task and transaction-event identities, their dates and any supported relationship between them. The shared word does not turn 7 September into the transaction's completion date.</p>
    <p>If glossary A were missing its definition, the correct review would first request it. That would be an evidence gap, not proof that the meanings must differ. In both cases the useful contribution names the subject, compared versions and consequence of substitution.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="common">Cooperation does not prove term equivalence</h2>
  <p>A strategic context map can describe who supplies shared meaning and how contexts cooperate. An individual term mapping makes a separate claim about defined concepts. A relationship between groups does not establish that every similarly named term is equivalent, and its arrow direction is not automatically the direction of a term correspondence.</p>
  <p>In candidate {candidateManifest.candidate_version}, the <a href="/development/property-pack/contexts#map">Common supply arrows</a> show shared elements supplied from the Common boundary. They are not mappings. The maintained register currently contains {contextMap.cross_domain_mappings.length} cross-domain mappings; its status is “{contextMap.mapping_status}”. The fictional bridges in this lesson must not be read as adopted additions to that register.</p>
  <p>Sometimes the best result is deliberate reuse. Sometimes it is a qualified connection that preserves a difference, or a decision not to map until evidence exists. The Interoperability Working Group's boundary work does not replace the domain owners' responsibility for internal meaning.</p>
  <p id="source-contract" class="learning-note">Use <a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-vocabularies-and-mappings">Review choices and mappings</a> to apply the comparison. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/context-map-records">context-map</a> and <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/mapping-records">mapping-record</a> references contain the separate formal contracts. Current boundary facts above come from the maintained candidate register, not from professional illustration or campaign copy.</p>
</ModellingLayout>
